{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2620","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2620","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2620\",\"parent_revision\":\"72a74bc6e5fad469d7150570433b10276624141a\",\"root_revision\":\"0c5ca2f651cb93b89b866333fc02bb29a8aed20b90e53f4e5f05f93fd2f04153\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nimplement-feature/SKILL.md fully enforces the autonomy_mode field: it reads status[\\\"autonomy\\\"] at the start of the progress loop and branches on it to determine dispatch topology (per_task \u2192 single sequential Agent call with user confirmation gate; full_auto \u2192 TeamCreate parallel batch; checkpoint \u2192 TeamCreate parallel batch with wave-confirmation gate). This is the primary lever controlling how many agents run concurrently and how much human oversight is applied.\\n\\nHowever no skill, reference file, or orchestrator sets this field based on any observable property of the work item. The field is declared in the SAM Plan Pydantic model with a default of \\\"full_auto\\\" (verified: sam_schema/core/models.py). Every plan therefore silently defaults to full_auto \u2014 maximum parallelism, no human gates \u2014 regardless of item type, complexity, scope, or risk.\\n\\nVerified by G7 complexity-routing layer (2026-06-08): consumption is fully enforced in implement-feature; who sets this field and what signals should determine its value is not attested anywhere in the skill files.\\n\\nImpact: the most powerful dispatch-shaping lever in the system is always set to its most aggressive value. A trivial one-file defect fix runs with the same topology as a complex unbounded-design feature spanning 20 files. The per_task and checkpoint modes exist but are unreachable without manual plan editing.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:40.433031Z</sub>\\n\\nClaims verified against primary sources:\\n\\n1. VERIFIED \u2014 `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` claim: `sam_schema/core/models.py` line 332\u2013333 reads `autonomy: Literal[\\\"full_auto\\\", \\\"checkpoint\\\", \\\"per_task\\\"] = Field(default=\\\"full_auto\\\", ...)`. SOURCE: direct Read of file at that line.\\n\\n2. VERIFIED \u2014 \\\"no skill sets this field\\\" claim: grep of all `*.md` files in `plugins/development-harness/skills/` for `autonomy` returns zero matches outside of `implement-feature/SKILL.md` and `TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md`. `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` and `generate-task/SKILL.md` contain no reference to autonomy. SOURCE: Grep run during grooming session 2026-06-09.\\n\\n3. VERIFIED \u2014 Enforcement in implement-feature: lines 41\u201393 contain the dispatch fork (`autonomy_mode == \\\"per_task\\\"` \u2192 single Agent, else TeamCreate); lines 248\u2013265 contain the Per-task Confirmation Gate; lines 272\u2013291 contain the Wave-Completion Confirmation Gate. SOURCE: direct Grep of implement-feature/SKILL.md with context.\\n\\n4. VERIFIED \u2014 G7 audit finding: `G7-complexity-routing.json` entry `G7.autonomy-mode` states: \\\"No skill file specifies who writes autonomy_mode to the plan or what item-level signals should determine its value.\\\" SOURCE: direct Read of file lines 40\u201346.\\n\\n5. VERIFIED \u2014 COVERAGE.md cross-reference: line 37 states \\\"G7 \u2014 autonomy_mode consumed but never set (backlog #2620)\\\". SOURCE: direct Read of COVERAGE.md line 37.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **autonomy_mode field is consumed by implement-feature but never set by any skill based on item characteristics \u2014 defaults silently to full_auto** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: G7 complexity-routing enforcement layer, G5 optionality layer, workflow-map audit 2026-06-08\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-08\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-09)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:30:24.560467Z</sub>\\n\\n6/10 \u2014 The missing-guardrail causes silent over-automation. The `per_task` and `checkpoint` modes exist and are fully enforced in implement-feature, but are unreachable without manual plan editing. High-complexity or high-risk items run with the same aggressive topology as trivial one-file fixes. Risk is elevated because the default is the most powerful setting, not the safest. No user-visible crash occurs, which masks the severity.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:30:57.561458Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: Items classified as `unbounded-design`, `missing-guardrail`, or `complexity: high` from receiving the oversight they warrant \u2014 they silently proceed with full parallel dispatch.\\n- Bottleneck: Human cannot insert oversight at plan-creation time without manually editing the plan YAML file before running implement-feature.\\n- Missing safety layer: A one-file defect fix and a 20-file unbounded-design feature receive identical dispatch topology \u2014 the risk-proportionate gating mechanism exists in code but is bypassed universally via Pydantic default.\\n- Scope creep risk: Under `full_auto`, all tasks in a wave launch simultaneously without any confirmation gate. For high-accuracy-risk items, this means multiple irreversible file changes may be committed before the human can review direction.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:31:29.933074Z</sub>\\n\\n- Enables proportional autonomy: trivial items run `full_auto`, complex or risky items get `checkpoint` or `per_task` gates automatically without manual intervention.\\n- Makes `per_task` and `checkpoint` modes reachable without YAML editing \u2014 currently these modes exist only in dead code from the workflow's perspective.\\n- Aligns the dispatch topology with the ARL human-touchpoint model already documented in `human-touchpoint-model.md` \u2014 closes the gap between the documented design intent and the actual behavior.\\n- Reduces the blast radius of miscalibrated AI execution on high-complexity work by inserting observable confirmation gates proportional to risk.\\n- Provides a documented, inspectable policy that developers can reason about \u2014 currently the \\\"policy\\\" is an invisible Pydantic default.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:04.444676Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen a SAM plan is created from a backlog item via the DH pipeline, the `autonomy` field on the plan should reflect the risk and complexity profile of the item:\\n\\n- `full_auto` \u2014 for items where all signals indicate low risk: `complexity: low`, `accuracy-risk: low`, `issue-classification: procedural` or `defect` with a bounded blast radius, priority P2 or Ideas.\\n- `checkpoint` \u2014 for items with moderate signals: `complexity: medium`, multi-file blast radius, or `issue-classification: feature` or `missing-guardrail`.\\n- `per_task` \u2014 for items with high-risk signals: `complexity: high`, `accuracy-risk: high`, `issue-classification: unbounded-design`, or blast radius exceeding a defined threshold (e.g. 10+ files or 3+ subsystems).\\n\\nThe assignment should happen at a defined call site in the planning workflow \u2014 either as a parameter to `sam_plan(action='create', autonomy=...)` or as an immediate post-creation `sam_plan(action='update', set_fields_json={\\\"autonomy\\\": ...})` call. The selecting skill or agent must read observable item metadata to derive the value \u2014 no manual editing of plan YAML should be required for items created via the DH pipeline.\\n\\nThe `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` on the Pydantic `Plan` model remains the backward-compatibility fallback for plans created outside the DH pipeline. It is not the intended production path for items worked through `/dh:work-backlog-item`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:37.040861Z</sub>\\n\\nA routing rule or policy in one of the planning-phase skills that:\\n\\n1. Reads item signals available at plan-creation time: `complexity`, `accuracy-risk`, `issue-classification`, blast radius estimate from grooming, and groomed priority.\\n2. Derives the appropriate `autonomy` mode from those signals using a documented, inspectable mapping.\\n3. Sets the `autonomy` field on the plan at creation time or immediately after creation via `sam_plan(action='update')`.\\n4. Is documented in a skill file such that a developer can read the policy and understand why a given item produces a given mode.\\n\\nThe policy lives in a skill file \u2014 not solely in Python code \u2014 because the selecting agent is an LLM reading skill instructions. The Pydantic `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` remains the in-code fallback for plans created outside the skill workflow.\\n\\nThe most natural call site is in `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` or the `swarm-task-planner` agent, at the point where `sam_plan(action='create')` is called. The `autonomy` argument is already accepted by `sam_plan create` \u2014 no schema changes are required.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:11.059093Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given a backlog item with `complexity: high` and `issue-classification: unbounded-design`, running the DH plan-creation workflow produces a plan where `autonomy` is `per_task` or `checkpoint` (not `full_auto`) \u2014 observable via `grep autonomy ~/.dh/projects/{slug}/plan/P*.yaml`.\\n2. Given a backlog item with `complexity: low` and `issue-classification: procedural`, the produced plan has `autonomy: full_auto` \u2014 the routing policy does not over-gate trivial work.\\n3. Given a backlog item whose groomed metadata lacks sufficient signals to determine autonomy (e.g. no `complexity` estimate, no `issue-classification`), the planning skill defaults to `checkpoint` (conservative) rather than silently defaulting to `full_auto`.\\n4. The routing policy is documented in a skill file (not only in code comments) such that reading the skill explains which signals map to which mode. A developer reading the skill can predict the output for a given item without running the workflow.\\n5. `mcp__plugin_dh_sam__sam_plan(config={\\\"action\\\": \\\"status\\\"}, plan=\\\"P{N}\\\")` on a plan created from a `complexity: high` item returns `\\\"autonomy\\\": \\\"per_task\\\"` or `\\\"autonomy\\\": \\\"checkpoint\\\"` \u2014 not `\\\"full_auto\\\"`.\\n6. The `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` on the Pydantic `Plan` model in `sam_schema/core/models.py` remains unchanged \u2014 backward compatibility for plans created outside the DH pipeline is preserved.\\n7. Running `/dh:implement-feature` on a plan with `autonomy: per_task` fires the Per-task Confirmation Gate after each task as documented in `implement-feature/SKILL.md` lines 248\u2013265 \u2014 confirming the production path connects to the existing enforcement.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:35:24.799015Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Primary source | `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/models.py` lines 328\u2013334 \u2014 `autonomy` field definition and Pydantic default |\\n| Skill | `/dh:implement-feature` \u2014 consumer; lines 41\u201393 (dispatch topology) and 248\u2013293 (gates) are the behavioral boundary |\\n| Skill | `/dh:add-new-feature` \u2014 plan creator; primary candidate for setter call site |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` entry `G7.autonomy-mode` \u2014 gap audit evidence |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` line 37 \u2014 coverage status |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` lines 204\u2013216 \u2014 autonomy field reference |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-architecture-diagram.md` lines 150\u2013171 \u2014 sam_plan status output shape including autonomy field |\\n| Reference | `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/human-touchpoint-model.md` \u2014 ARL escalation signals that parallel the autonomy routing decision |\\n| Backlog | #2619 \u2014 issue type routing does not propagate downstream (same root-cause class) |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:35:42.064785Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 the `autonomy` field, all three mode behaviors in implement-feature, and the `sam_plan(action='update')` MCP tool are already implemented. The schema is stable. This item only adds the setter logic.\\n- Blocks: None directly. Related to #2619 (issue type routing downstream) \u2014 both address the same class of problem where grooming signals are captured but not used to route downstream execution. They are independent and can be worked in parallel.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:35:59.957155Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The `autonomy` field, Pydantic model, and all three mode behaviors in implement-feature are already implemented. The work is: (1) design the signal-to-mode mapping policy (requires a design decision about thresholds), (2) identify and confirm the exact call site in `add-new-feature` or a downstream planning skill where the field is set, (3) write the skill instruction that performs the derivation, (4) update reference docs, (5) verify the production path produces the expected mode for representative items. No schema changes required. Complexity comes from designing the right policy and confirming signal availability at plan-creation time rather than from implementation difficulty.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:10.693405Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (missing-guardrail)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The `autonomy` field on a SAM Plan, as read by `implement-feature` at the start of the Progress Loop. The field determines whether the plan executes as fully autonomous (`full_auto`), wave-gated (`checkpoint`), or task-by-task with human confirmation (`per_task`).\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that observable item signals (complexity, risk, issue classification, blast radius) produce a non-default `autonomy` value on new plans when those signals indicate the plan warrants human oversight.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:36:19.711762Z</sub>\\n\\nThe DH harness design intent (from `CLAUDE.md` and `human-touchpoint-model.md`) is that human escalation follows ARL constraint analysis \u2014 not arbitrary checkpoints. The `autonomy` field is the implement-feature analog of this principle: dispatch topology should follow risk analysis, not a hard-coded default.\\n\\nThe current state inverts the design intent: the most aggressive execution mode is the universal default. The design documents (`human-touchpoint-model.md`, `complexity-fit-and-economics-of-agents.md`) describe a risk-proportionate model; the skill pipeline does not implement it.\\n\\nThis item closes that gap by wiring the existing signal infrastructure (complexity, accuracy-risk, issue-classification from grooming) to the existing enforcement infrastructure (autonomy mode gates in implement-feature) through a documented routing policy. No new concepts are introduced \u2014 only the missing connection between inputs and outputs.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:50.936477Z</sub>\\n\\nFiles that need to change to implement this item:\\n\\n| File | Change type | Reason |\\n|------|-------------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` (or `swarm-task-planner` agent) | Add routing policy | This is where `sam_plan(action='create')` is called \u2014 the `autonomy` argument must be derived and passed here |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` | Update reference docs | Add documentation of which signals map to which autonomy mode \u2014 currently has field definition but no setter guidance |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` | Update required reading | The `autonomy` field note (line 394) already mentions it but does not document the selection policy |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` | Update enforcement_status | Change `G7.autonomy-mode` from `partially-enforced` to `enforced` once the setter is implemented |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` | Update status | Change from `LOGGED, needs groom` to `COMPLETE` |\\n\\nFiles that must NOT change:\\n\\n| File | Reason |\\n|------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/models.py` | `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` must remain \u2014 backward compatibility for plans created outside DH pipeline |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | Consumption logic is correct and complete; no changes needed |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:16.209206Z</sub>\\n\\n- Classification: `missing-guardrail`\\n- Rationale: The field that controls human oversight over agent dispatch exists, is enforced at consumption, and has been set to its most permissive value (`full_auto`) as a Pydantic default. No production code or skill logic selects a more conservative value based on item risk. The guardrail (per_task / checkpoint modes) exists architecturally but is never activated in the normal workflow path.\\n- Root cause: The production side of the `autonomy` field was never implemented. The consumption side was built first (implement-feature enforcement), but the setter \u2014 the skill or planner that should read item signals and assign the value \u2014 was not added. The gap was first identified in the G7 workflow audit on 2026-06-08.\\n- Pattern: This is the same class of problem as #2619 (issue type routing does not propagate downstream). In both cases, a signal is captured during grooming but never used to alter downstream execution topology.\\n- Risk profile: Silent \u2014 no error is raised, no warning is emitted. The system operates normally but at a uniformly aggressive automation level regardless of item risk.\\n</div>\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:52.906530Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Determine what observable signals should drive `autonomy_mode` selection at plan-creation time, and where in the skill pipeline the field should be set.\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - `autonomy` field defined in `sam_schema/core/models.py` line 332 with `default=\\\"full_auto\\\"` and `Literal[\\\"full_auto\\\", \\\"checkpoint\\\", \\\"per_task\\\"]` type constraint \u2014 confirmed by primary source read.\\n  - `implement-feature/SKILL.md` fully enforces all three autonomy modes at lines 41\u201393 (dispatch topology) and 248\u2013293 (Per-task and Wave-Completion gates) \u2014 confirmed.\\n  - G7 workflow-layer audit (2026-06-08) confirms the gap: consumption fully enforced; production not governed by any skill logic. Source: `docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` entry `G7.autonomy-mode`.\\n  - Observable signals exist on the backlog item and plan: `complexity` (low/medium/high on Task), `accuracy-risk`, `issue-classification` (procedural/defect/recurring-pattern/missing-guardrail/unbounded-design), groomed priority (P0/P1/P2/Ideas), blast-radius from Impact Radius.\\n  - `add-new-feature` delegates plan creation to `swarm-task-planner` via `sam_plan(action='create')` \u2014 this is where `autonomy` could be set.\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Mapping from item signals to autonomy mode \u2014 can be derived from existing ARL human-touchpoint model and the complexity-fit model in `planner-rt-ica/SKILL.md`. The derivation logic is not present in any skill but all component signals are available and enumerated.\\n  - Which skill owns the write responsibility \u2014 derivable by tracing who calls `sam_plan(action='create')` and who could call `sam_plan(action='update', set_fields_json={\\\"autonomy\\\": ...})`.\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Authoritative policy for which signal combination maps to which autonomy mode. This is a design decision, not a code search.\\n  - Whether `add-new-feature` should pass `autonomy` at creation time or a post-creation skill should update it via `sam_plan(action='update')`.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:53.920277Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Pick any backlog item with `complexity: high` or `issue-classification: unbounded-design` or a large blast radius (10+ files).\\n2. Run `/dh:work-backlog-item #N` through to plan creation (stop after `sam_plan(action='create')` returns).\\n3. Inspect the resulting plan YAML: `grep autonomy ~/.dh/projects/{slug}/plan/P*.yaml`\\n4. Observe: `autonomy: full_auto` regardless of item characteristics.\\n5. Run `/dh:implement-feature #N` and observe that it dispatches all tasks in parallel via TeamCreate with no human gate, regardless of item risk level.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ff2410b4461204c19e125912311d839121702cd30f28bc1d207d8ae845de9454\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg1d3Q\"}"}